<GuideLayout
  title="Sleeping bag temperature ratings, explained"
  description="How to read comfort and limit figures, then leave a sensible margin for the conditions and the way you sleep."
  eyebrow="Sleep system"
  readTime="6 min read"
>
  <h2>Start with the comfort figure</h2><p>
    Where a bag has standardised ratings, the comfort rating is the more useful
    starting point for many buyers. A lower-limit figure usually assumes a
    warmer sleeper in a curled position and should not be treated as a promise
    of a comfortable night.
  </p>
  <h2>Match the bag to the overnight low</h2><p>
    Look at the likely temperature at your actual campsite, including elevation
    and local exposure. Forecasts for a nearby town can be much warmer than a
    high or sheltered valley campsite. Add a margin rather than choosing a bag
    rated exactly to the expected low.
  </p>
  <h2>Your mat is part of the rating</h2><p>
    Temperature testing assumes an insulated mat and specific clothing. On cold
    ground, an under-insulated mat can make a warm sleeping bag feel inadequate.
    Treat the bag, mat and sleep clothing as one system.
  </p>
  <h2>Allow for personal variation</h2><p>
    People sleep at different temperatures. Fatigue, food intake, damp clothing,
    wind exposure and hydration can all affect how warm you feel. If you already
    know you sleep cold, choose more margin.
  </p>
  <h2>Down or synthetic?</h2><p>
    Down generally offers excellent warmth for its weight and packs small, but
    it often costs more and needs careful moisture management. Synthetic
    insulation is commonly cheaper and more tolerant of dampness, though it
    tends to be bulkier and heavier for similar warmth.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
